by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-17T16:07:16Z

Thanks for your patience on this one, and for pushing back rather than adding three transports. You were right that the routing key is not reachable at decode time: `Serializer::decode()` needs `headers['type']` and `PhpSerializer` needs a serialized envelope, both before any middleware runs, so the `AmqpReceivedStamp` route cannot help here.

I rebased it onto 8.2 and pushed. Your commit could not be replayed, since `getEnvelope()` gained `MessageDecodingFailedException::wrap()`, fetch size and keepalive in the meantime, so I rewrote it against the current code and kept your authorship.

Three changes to what you had:

`routingKey` is now `routing_key`. The other keys in that array are lowercase, and the bridge already spells it that way in `default_publish_routing_key` and the `%routing_key%` queue pattern.

`extra` is now documented on `SerializerInterface::decode()`, since it is part of a public contract. The wording says what it is and what it is not: metadata added by the receiving transport, not produced by `encode()`, does not survive a round trip, so implementations must treat it as optional.

It is also documented on `MessageTypeAwareSerializerInterface::getMessageType()`, which did not exist when you opened this. That method lets a serializer name the message class without instantiating the payload, and `SigningSerializer` uses it to decide whether a message required a signature. It receives the same array you are extending, so `extra` reaches it too. I added a `SHOULD NOT` against deriving the type from `extra` there: the signature covers the encoded envelope, so a type taken from transport metadata is not covered by one.

I also considered injecting the routing key as a header instead, which would have needed no interface change. I did not, because no receiver in the tree fabricates a header today: `headers` is exactly what the sender put on the wire. Injecting one would either overwrite a header the publisher legitimately set, or be indistinguishable from one they forged, and `getMessageType()` could not tell the two apart. Keeping it under `extra` makes the receiver the only writer.

Amqp bridge suite is green at 110 tests. Over to reviewers for the shape of `extra` itself.
